      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Research consistently indicates that building new highways in urban and suburban areas has little to no impact on reducing traffic congestion and is often replaced by \"induced demand,\" causing new roads to quickly fill back up. While rural economic mobility and emergency access remain critical justifications for limited road improvements, the dominant consensus among transportation economists is that this era of massive new highway construction is largely outdated compared to the proven efficiency and land-saver benefits of intelligent transportation systems and better connected public transit.",
