    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely correct to push back on that absolute framing; presenting choices as A vs. B is far more appropriate for a balanced municipal report. In reality, the consensus isn't that cars are \"failed,\" but rather that specific \"greenfield\" highway expansion is the wrong tool for current urban challenges compared to optimizing transit or \"intelligent\" road systems. The strongest argument against *new* roads is usually the \"induced demand\" and \"land value\" issues, not a rejection of all road safety, emergency access, or all rural connectivity needs.\n\nTherefore, your strategy should likely position \"highways vs. transit\" as \"appropriately funded and connected infrastructure\" versus \"undifferentiated expansion,\" or \"multi-modal integration\" versus \"single-mode reliance.\"",
      "source": "target",
